WEBVTT

00:00.590 --> 00:03.240
So we have got a very
interesting scenario here.

00:03.240 --> 00:08.730
I have this conical thimble-like
cup that is 4 centimeters high.

00:08.730 --> 00:11.450
And also, the diameter
of the top of the cup

00:11.450 --> 00:13.450
is also 4 centimeters.

00:13.450 --> 00:16.480
And I'm pouring water
into this cup right now.

00:16.480 --> 00:20.560
And I'm pouring the water at
a rate of 1 cubic centimeter.

00:20.560 --> 00:22.800
1 cubic centimeter per second.

00:22.800 --> 00:26.130
And right at this
moment, there is a height

00:26.130 --> 00:28.930
of 2 centimeters of water
in the cup right now.

00:28.930 --> 00:32.070
So the height right now from the
bottom of the cup to this point

00:32.070 --> 00:34.230
right over here
is 2 centimeters.

00:34.230 --> 00:36.980
So my question to you
is, at what rate--

00:36.980 --> 00:39.700
we know the rate at which the
water is flowing into the cup,

00:39.700 --> 00:43.390
we're being given
a volume per time.

00:43.390 --> 00:45.550
My question to you is
right at this moment,

00:45.550 --> 00:47.270
right when we are
filling our cup

00:47.270 --> 00:49.660
at 1 cubic centimeter
per second.

00:49.660 --> 00:51.910
And we have exactly
2 centimeters

00:51.910 --> 00:55.200
of water in the cup,
2 centimeters deep

00:55.200 --> 00:57.580
of water in the cup,
what is the rate at which

00:57.580 --> 01:01.770
the height of the
water is changing?

01:01.770 --> 01:05.540
What is the rate at which
this height right over here

01:05.540 --> 01:06.440
is actually changing?

01:06.440 --> 01:10.902
We know it's 2 centimeters,
but how fast is it changing?

01:10.902 --> 01:12.610
Well let's think about
this a little bit.

01:12.610 --> 01:13.877
What are we being given?

01:13.877 --> 01:15.960
We're given-- we are being
given the rate at which

01:15.960 --> 01:19.530
the volume of the water is
changing with respect to time.

01:19.530 --> 01:20.740
So let's write that down.

01:20.740 --> 01:24.290
We are being given the rate at
which the volume of the water

01:24.290 --> 01:26.900
is changing with
respect to time,

01:26.900 --> 01:34.630
and we're told that this is 1
cubic centimeter per second.

01:34.630 --> 01:36.440
And what are we
trying to figure out?

01:36.440 --> 01:38.810
Well we're trying to
figure out how fast

01:38.810 --> 01:41.330
the height of the water is
changing with respect to time.

01:41.330 --> 01:43.550
We know that the height
right now is 2 centimeters.

01:43.550 --> 01:45.560
But what we want to
figure out is the rate

01:45.560 --> 01:50.300
at which the height is
changing with respect to time.

01:50.300 --> 01:52.060
If we can figure
out this, then we

01:52.060 --> 01:54.880
have essentially
answered the question.

01:54.880 --> 01:56.620
So one way that we
can do this is we

01:56.620 --> 01:59.530
can come up with the
relationship between the volume

01:59.530 --> 02:02.704
at any moment in time and the
height at any moment in time.

02:02.704 --> 02:04.120
And then maybe
take the derivative

02:04.120 --> 02:06.870
of that relationship,
possibly using the chain rule,

02:06.870 --> 02:09.684
to come up with a relationship
between the rate at which

02:09.684 --> 02:12.100
the volume is changing and the
rate at which the height is

02:12.100 --> 02:13.250
changing.

02:13.250 --> 02:14.900
So let's try to do
it step by step.

02:14.900 --> 02:17.024
So first of all, can we
come up with a relationship

02:17.024 --> 02:21.220
between the volume and the
height at any given moment?

02:21.220 --> 02:23.550
Well we have also
been given the formula

02:23.550 --> 02:25.690
for the volume of a
cone right over here.

02:25.690 --> 02:28.480
The volume of a cone
is 1/3 times the area

02:28.480 --> 02:30.485
of the base of the
cone, times the height.

02:30.485 --> 02:32.110
And we won't prove
it here, although we

02:32.110 --> 02:33.110
could prove it later on.

02:33.110 --> 02:36.330
Especially when we start
doing solids of revolutions

02:36.330 --> 02:38.700
within in integral calculus.

02:38.700 --> 02:40.450
But we'll just take
it on faith right now,

02:40.450 --> 02:44.340
that this is how we can figure
out the volume of a cone.

02:44.340 --> 02:47.280
So given this can we
figure out volume--

02:47.280 --> 02:49.710
can we figure out an
expression that relates volume

02:49.710 --> 02:51.470
to the height of the cone?

02:51.470 --> 02:53.200
Well we could say
that volume-- and I'll

02:53.200 --> 02:56.380
do it in this blue color--
the volume of water

02:56.380 --> 02:57.980
is what we really care about.

02:57.980 --> 03:00.740
The volume of water
is going to be

03:00.740 --> 03:07.950
equal to 1/3 times the area
of the surface of the water--

03:07.950 --> 03:16.890
area of water surface-- times
our height of the water.

03:16.890 --> 03:18.710
So times h.

03:18.710 --> 03:21.010
So how can we figure out the
area of the water surface,

03:21.010 --> 03:23.320
preferably in terms of h?

03:23.320 --> 03:25.290
Well we see right over
here, the diameter

03:25.290 --> 03:27.920
across the top of the
cone is 4 centimeters.

03:27.920 --> 03:31.840
And the height of the
whole cup is 4 centimeters.

03:31.840 --> 03:34.600
And so that ratio is
going to be true of any--

03:34.600 --> 03:36.100
at any depth of water.

03:36.100 --> 03:39.100
It's always going to have the
same ratio between the diameter

03:39.100 --> 03:41.140
across the top and the height.

03:41.140 --> 03:43.230
Because these are
lines right over here.

03:43.230 --> 03:47.290
So at any given point, the
ratio between this and this

03:47.290 --> 03:48.420
is going to be the same.

03:48.420 --> 03:50.530
So at any given
point, the diameter

03:50.530 --> 03:53.500
across the surface of the
water-- if the depth is h,

03:53.500 --> 03:55.370
the diameter across the
surface of the water

03:55.370 --> 03:57.690
is also going to be h.

03:57.690 --> 03:59.112
And so from that
we can figure out

03:59.112 --> 04:00.570
what are the radius
is going to be.

04:00.570 --> 04:02.730
The radius is going
to be h over 2.

04:02.730 --> 04:05.060
And so the area of
the water surface

04:05.060 --> 04:10.500
is going to be pi r squared,
pi times the radius squared.

04:10.500 --> 04:13.490
h over 2 squared.

04:13.490 --> 04:15.670
That's the area of the
surface of the water.

04:15.670 --> 04:17.810
And of course we still
have the 1/3 out here.

04:17.810 --> 04:21.980
And we're still multiplying
by this h over here.

04:21.980 --> 04:23.900
So let me see if I
can simplify this.

04:23.900 --> 04:33.530
So this gives us 1/3 times pi h
squared over 4 times another h,

04:33.530 --> 04:42.350
which is equal to-- we have pi,
h to the third power over 12.

04:42.350 --> 04:45.600
So that is our volume.

04:45.600 --> 04:48.930
Now what we want to do is relate
the volume, how fast the volume

04:48.930 --> 04:50.510
is changing with
respect to time,

04:50.510 --> 04:53.210
and how fast the height is
changing with respect to time.

04:53.210 --> 04:55.877
So we care with respect to
time, since we care so much

04:55.877 --> 04:57.710
about what's happening
with respect to time,

04:57.710 --> 05:00.290
let's take the derivative of
both sides of this equation

05:00.290 --> 05:01.529
with respect to time.

05:01.529 --> 05:03.820
To do that, and just so I
have enough space to do that,

05:03.820 --> 05:05.240
let me move this over.

05:05.240 --> 05:08.420
Let me move this over to
the right a little bit.

05:08.420 --> 05:10.580
So I just move this
over to the right.

05:10.580 --> 05:13.310
And so now we can take the
derivative with respect

05:13.310 --> 05:16.350
to time of both sides
of this business.

05:16.350 --> 05:21.010
So the derivative with
respect to time of our volume

05:21.010 --> 05:24.839
and the derivative with respect
to time of this business.

05:24.839 --> 05:27.130
Well the derivative with
respect to time of our volume,

05:27.130 --> 05:30.850
we could just rewrite that as dV
dt, this thing right over here.

05:30.850 --> 05:36.850
This is dV dt, and
this is going to be

05:36.850 --> 05:39.404
equal to-- well we could
take the constants out

05:39.404 --> 05:41.320
of this-- this is going
to be equal to pi over

05:41.320 --> 05:52.300
12 times the derivative
with respect to t of h, of h

05:52.300 --> 05:55.620
to the third power.

05:55.620 --> 05:57.930
And just so that the
next few things I do

05:57.930 --> 05:59.539
will appear a
little bit clearer,

05:59.539 --> 06:01.580
we're assuming that height
is a function of time.

06:01.580 --> 06:02.780
In fact, it's definitely
a function of time.

06:02.780 --> 06:04.750
As time goes on, the
height will change.

06:04.750 --> 06:06.960
Because we're pouring
more and more water here.

06:06.960 --> 06:09.210
So instead of just writing
h to the third power, which

06:09.210 --> 06:12.770
I could write over here,
let me write h of t

06:12.770 --> 06:13.620
to the third power.

06:13.620 --> 06:16.822
Just to make it clear that
this is a function of t.

06:16.822 --> 06:21.160
h of t to the third power.

06:21.160 --> 06:23.430
Now what is the derivative
with respect to t,

06:23.430 --> 06:26.300
of h of t to the third power.

06:26.300 --> 06:28.190
Now, you might be getting
a tingling feeling

06:28.190 --> 06:31.710
that the chain rule
might be applicable here.

06:31.710 --> 06:33.846
So let's think about
the chain rule.

06:33.846 --> 06:36.220
The chain rule tells us-- let
me rewrite everything else.

06:36.220 --> 06:43.070
dV with respect to t, is going
to be equal to pi over 12,

06:43.070 --> 06:45.320
times the derivative of
this with respect to t.

06:45.320 --> 06:47.570
If we want to take the
derivative of this with respect

06:47.570 --> 06:49.630
to t, we have something
to the third power.

06:49.630 --> 06:52.580
So we want to take the
derivative of something

06:52.580 --> 06:54.750
to the third power with
respect to something.

06:54.750 --> 06:56.670
So that's going to
be-- let me write

06:56.670 --> 06:59.350
this in a different color,
maybe in orange-- so that's

06:59.350 --> 07:06.460
going to be 3 times
our something squared,

07:06.460 --> 07:11.840
times the derivative of that
something, with respect to t.

07:11.840 --> 07:21.770
Times dh-- I've already used
that pink-- times dh dt.

07:21.770 --> 07:23.250
Let's just be very clear.

07:23.250 --> 07:25.610
This orange term right
over here-- and I'm

07:25.610 --> 07:32.760
just using the chain rule--
this is the derivative of h of t

07:32.760 --> 07:38.550
to the third power
with respect to h of t.

07:38.550 --> 07:41.990
And then we're going to multiply
that times the derivative of h

07:41.990 --> 07:46.520
of t with respect to t.

07:46.520 --> 07:48.290
And then that gives
us the derivative

07:48.290 --> 07:52.081
of this entire thing, h of t to
the third power, with respect

07:52.081 --> 07:52.580
to t.

07:52.580 --> 07:56.800
This will give us
the derivative of h

07:56.800 --> 08:03.630
of t to the third power with
respect to d with respect

08:03.630 --> 08:05.090
to t, which is
exactly what we want

08:05.090 --> 08:06.740
to do when we apply
this operator.

08:06.740 --> 08:08.480
How fast is this changing?

08:08.480 --> 08:11.510
How is this changing
with respect to time?

08:11.510 --> 08:13.172
So we can just
rewrite this, just

08:13.172 --> 08:14.380
so gets a little bit cleaner.

08:14.380 --> 08:15.879
Let me rewrite
everything I've done.

08:15.879 --> 08:18.640
So we've got dV, the rate
at which our volume is

08:18.640 --> 08:20.570
changing with respect to time.

08:20.570 --> 08:24.050
The rate at which our volume is
changing with respect to time

08:24.050 --> 08:31.460
is equal to pi over 12
times 3 h of t squared,

08:31.460 --> 08:36.289
or I could just write
that as 3h squared,

08:36.289 --> 08:38.929
times the rate at which
the height is changing

08:38.929 --> 08:41.960
with respect to
time, times dh dt.

08:47.222 --> 08:48.680
And you might be
a little confused.

08:48.680 --> 08:52.670
You might have been tempted to
take the derivative over here

08:52.670 --> 08:53.607
with respect to h.

08:53.607 --> 08:55.190
But remember, we're
thinking about how

08:55.190 --> 08:57.890
things are changing
with respect to time.

08:57.890 --> 09:00.010
So we're assuming--
we did express volume

09:00.010 --> 09:01.820
as a function of
height-- but we're

09:01.820 --> 09:03.820
saying that height itself
is a function of time.

09:03.820 --> 09:05.653
So we're taking the
derivative of everything

09:05.653 --> 09:06.790
with respect to time.

09:06.790 --> 09:08.900
So that's why the chain
rule came into play

09:08.900 --> 09:10.690
when we were taking
the derivative of h,

09:10.690 --> 09:12.740
or the derivative of
h of t, because we're

09:12.740 --> 09:15.230
assuming that h is
a function of time.

09:15.230 --> 09:18.740
Now what does this thing
right over here get us?

09:18.740 --> 09:20.660
Well we're telling us
at the exact moment

09:20.660 --> 09:22.880
that we set up this
problem, we know

09:22.880 --> 09:27.900
what dV dt is, we know that it's
1 centimeter cubed per second.

09:27.900 --> 09:29.530
We know that this
right over here

09:29.530 --> 09:33.620
is 1 centimeter
cubed per second.

09:33.620 --> 09:35.620
We know what our height
is right at this moment.

09:35.620 --> 09:37.090
We were told it
is 2 centimeters.

09:43.240 --> 09:45.200
So the only unknown
we have over here

09:45.200 --> 09:46.750
is the rate at
which our height is

09:46.750 --> 09:48.070
changing with respect to time.

09:48.070 --> 09:50.320
Which is exactly what
we needed to figure out

09:50.320 --> 09:51.834
in the first place.

09:51.834 --> 09:53.250
So we just have
to solve for that.

09:53.250 --> 09:55.870
So we get 1 cubic
centimeter-- let

09:55.870 --> 10:01.220
me make it clear-- we get 1
cubic centimeter per second--

10:01.220 --> 10:03.520
I won't write the units
to save some space-- is

10:03.520 --> 10:05.306
equal to pi over 2.

10:05.306 --> 10:06.930
And I'll write this
in a neutral color.

10:06.930 --> 10:08.638
Actually, let me write
in the same color.

10:08.638 --> 10:15.480
Is equal to pi over 2,
times 3, times h squared.

10:15.480 --> 10:18.616
h is 2 so you're going to get
4 squared centimeters if we

10:18.616 --> 10:19.420
kept the units.

10:19.420 --> 10:21.674
So 3 times 4.

10:21.674 --> 10:23.340
All right let me be
careful, that wasn't

10:23.340 --> 10:25.765
pi over 2, that was pi over 12.

10:25.765 --> 10:29.780
This is a pi over
12 right over here.

10:29.780 --> 10:39.350
So you get pi over 12, times 3
times 2 squared, times dh dt.

10:39.350 --> 10:41.020
All of this is equal to 1.

10:41.020 --> 10:42.920
So now I'll switch
to a neutral color.

10:42.920 --> 10:46.130
We get 1 is equal to,
well 3 times 4 is 12,

10:46.130 --> 10:47.710
cancels out with that 12.

10:47.710 --> 10:51.800
We get one is equal
to pi times dh dt.

10:51.800 --> 10:54.770
To solve for dh dt
divide both sides by pi.

10:54.770 --> 10:57.162
And we get our drum roll now.

10:57.162 --> 10:59.370
The rate at which our height
is changing with respect

10:59.370 --> 11:03.260
to time as we're putting 1 cubic
centimeter of water per second

11:03.260 --> 11:03.910
in.

11:03.910 --> 11:05.987
And right when our height
is 2 centimeters high,

11:05.987 --> 11:08.570
the rate at which this height
is changing with respect to time

11:08.570 --> 11:13.330
is 1 over pi.

11:13.330 --> 11:15.330
And I haven't done the
dimensional analysis

11:15.330 --> 11:19.486
but this is going to be
in centimeters per second.

11:19.486 --> 11:21.360
You can work through
the dimensional analysis

11:21.360 --> 11:24.250
if you like by putting in the
dimensions right over here.

11:24.250 --> 11:25.610
But there you have it.

11:25.610 --> 11:27.890
That's how fast
our height is going

11:27.890 --> 11:31.790
to be changing at
exactly that moment.
